1 NOTE {geni:about_me} LOUIS F. HEINZE DIES SUDDENLY Well Known Lewiston Resident Succumbs to Extended Illness. HAS SERVED AS CHAIRMAN
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Heinze, a prominent resident of the town of lewiston, died suddenly at the Portage hospital Tuesday night after an extended illness of heart disease. His condition had been serious for several months past, and although he ha
2 CONC d been able to be about most of the time, his health gradually declined. Tuesday night he was taken with a sinking spell while about his room at the hospital and in falling to the floor he struck a window, cutting his face conside
2 CONC rably. Dr. Thomson was promptly on hand but it was found that death had been instaneous. The remains were removed to the Murlson chapel and from there were taken to the family home in the town of Lewiston. Mr. Heinze was born in G
2 CONC ermany 60 years ago. He came to America in 1870, preceding his parents, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Heinze, one year to this country. He settled in Lewiston, his parents following, and the family for many years was one of the most highly e
2 CONC steemed in that vicinity. Mr. Heinze has been honored repeatedly by public offices at the hands of his fellow townsmen and up to his last illness had been serving as chairman of the town of Lewiston. His wife and eight children su
2 CONC rvive. The children are Fred, Gustave, William, Otto, Louis, and Anna at home; Mrs. Lee, Tomah; Mrs. Doyle, Milwaukee; and Mrs. Oleson, Briggsville. Two sisters, Mrs. Charles Schmidt of Pardeeville and Mrs. August Copplin of Caled
2 CONC onia and two brothers, Leopold Heinze and Charles Heinze of Pacific also survive him.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Posted to Columbia County Obits -March 17, 2001
